Calc: an .xlsx import keeps the plain entries of the shared string table as pooled strings, not RichString objects.

The import read every shared string table entry into a RichString object with a vector of portions, also the plain
ones, and kept them all until the sheets were read; every cell then put its entry's text into the document's string
pool again. On a sheet of 5.3 million text cells over 1.2 million entries these objects took about 160 MB at the
load peak, allocated between the long-lived entry texts, and after they were freed the allocator kept the pages:
the open document held about 175 MB more RSS (171 MB free inside the glibc arenas) than the same sheet written
with inline strings. An entry that is plain whatever the cell's font (one portion without a font, no phonetic
text, not empty, no line break) is now pooled as soon as it is read and its RichString dropped before the next
entry, so the temporaries reuse the same memory. The cells of the entry take the pooled string; a cell whose
font needs rich text (superscript/subscript) gets a RichString made from the text, exactly as before. The
environment variable HST_SC_XLSX_SST_PLAIN_OFF keeps every entry as a RichString.

New logic in sc/source/filter/inc/hstsstplainstrings.hxx (HST). Hooks: sharedstringsbuffer.hxx/.cxx (compact the
last entry before the next one and at the end, null check, fallback in getString), sheetdatabuffer.cxx (plain
fast path), richstring.hxx (font id getter), documentimport.hxx/.cxx (setStringCell for an already pooled string).

License: modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0); the new header is HST-proprietary.
Test: CppunitTest_sc_subsequent_filters_test5 testXlsxPlainSharedStringsPooledWhenRead loads the same file on both
paths and compares every cell: type, text and pooled string identity (spaces, escapes, empty, rich, multi-line,
duplicate text, missing index, superscript cell font).
